        {
          "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/services/tooth-extraction#faq",
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "isPartOf": {
            "@id": "https://www.livewelldentalva.com/services/tooth-extraction#webpage"
          },
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "You may need an extraction if the tooth is severely damaged, infected, loose, or cannot be restored in a predictable way. We will examine the tooth and explain your options first. If the tooth can still be saved, treatment such as a root canal may be discussed before extraction."
              },
              "name": "How do I know if my tooth needs to be removed?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The area is numbed before treatment, and we focus on keeping you comfortable throughout the visit. You may feel pressure, but you should not feel sharp pain during the procedure."
              },
              "name": "Does a tooth extraction hurt?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Most patients begin feeling better within a few days. Full healing takes longer, depending on the tooth, the extraction site, and your overall health."
              },
              "name": "How long does it take to recover?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Soft foods are best at first. Yogurt, eggs, mashed potatoes, smoothies without a straw, applesauce, and lukewarm soup are common options during early healing."
              },
              "name": "What should I eat after an extraction?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "It depends on the tooth and your bite. Many extracted teeth should be replaced to protect chewing function, spacing, and bone health. We will explain your options clearly, including dental implants and dental bridges when appropriate."
              },
              "name": "Will I need to replace the tooth after extraction?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "In many cases, yes. Sometimes an implant can be planned soon after extraction. In other cases, the area may need healing or bone grafting first. You can learn more about replacement options on our dental implants page."
              },
              "name": "Can I get an implant after a tooth extraction?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes, sometimes. If the tooth still has enough healthy structure and the infection or damage can be treated predictably, Dr. Chris may recommend saving it. Depending on the situation, options may include a root canal, dental crown, or another restorative treatment."
              },
              "name": "Can a tooth be saved instead of removed?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Not exactly. A tooth extraction can involve any tooth that is damaged, infected, loose, or causing problems. Wisdom teeth removal is a specific type of extraction for third molars, often when they are impacted, painful, or crowding nearby teeth. Learn more about wisdom teeth removal"
              },
              "name": "Is tooth extraction the same as wisdom teeth removal?"
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "A tooth extraction typically runs $300 to $450. Simple extractions for a visible tooth generally cost less, while impacted or more complex extractions run toward the higher end."
              },
              "name": "How much does a tooth extraction cost?"
            }
          ]
